    <Fragment slot="aim">
      <p>The Dignity for Dead Women campaign is led by Level Up to challenge harmful and inaccurate media reporting on fatal domestic abuse and domestic homicide.</p>
      <p>Every three days in the UK, a woman is killed by a partner or ex-partner. Media coverage often misrepresents these killings as isolated incidents or "crimes of passion", rather than the predictable outcome of sustained coercive control.</p>
      <p>Dignity for Dead Women calls for accurate, responsible and dignified reporting of fatal domestic abuse to prevent further deaths and improve public understanding of domestic abuse.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="why">
      <p>Every three days, a woman is murdered by her partner or ex-partner. The way the media reports on these deaths directly influences public understandings of domestic abuse – and how we can prevent it.</p>
      <p>When a woman is killed by someone she's been in a relationship with, her death marks the endpoint to a sustained period of coercive control. Accurate and dignified reporting of these killings is essential to preventing more deaths. In the same way journalists understand they have a responsibility to report suicides with care, Level Up calls for fatal domestic abuse to be treated in the same way.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="story-heading">How the Media guidelines Campaign began</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="story">
      <p>The Level Up team read the newspapers every day, scanning for news on gender justice in the UK. In the summer of 2018, the team noticed several cases of fatal domestic abuse being reported in the press in an irresponsible, inaccurate way. With headlines citing "spurned lovers" and "jealous rages", framing acts of abuse as "crimes of passion" rather than crimes of control, the media was actively reinforcing the myths that keep killing women.</p>
      <p>There are strict media guidelines on reporting suicide. Fatal domestic abuse is an equally serious and preventable public health problem – but is too often seen as a "private" issue. Level Up believes that every article is an opportunity to prevent further deaths, so we set about trying to improve the way fatal domestic abuse is reported – both to provide dignity and justice for families, and to share accurate public health information.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </CampaignTemplate>
